{"name":"Pier 17 Steel","hex":"#647d8e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pier","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A raised platform built from the shore out over water, supported on piles; used to secure, or provide access to shipping; a jetty.","A similar structure, especially at a seaside resort, used to provide entertainment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pier"},{"term":"steel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An artificial metal produced from iron, harder and more elastic than elemental iron; used figuratively as a symbol of hardness.","Any item made of this metal, particularly including:\nBladed or pointed weapons, as swords, javelins, daggers."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of steel.","Similar to steel in color, strength, or the like; steely."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/steel"}],"entries":[{"title":"South Street Seaport","blocks":[{"text":"The South Street Seaport is a historic area and neighborhood in the New York City borough of Manhattan, centered where Fulton Street meets the East River, within the Financial District of Lower Manhattan. The Seaport is a designated historic district. It is part of Manhattan Community Board 1 and is next to the East River to the southeast and the Two Bridges neighborhood to the northeast."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/South_Street_Seaport%2C_Detroit_Photographic_Company_%280616%29.jpg/330px-South_Street_Seaport%2C_Detroit_Photographic_Company_%280616%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Street_Seaport","wikidata":"Q223000"},{"title":"Pier","blocks":[{"text":"A pier is a raised structure that rises above a body of water and usually juts out from its shore, typically supported by piles or pillars, and provides above-water access to offshore areas. Frequent pier uses include fishing, boat docking and access for both passengers and cargo, and oceanside recreation such as swimming. Bridges, buildings, and walkways may all be supported by architectural piers. Their open structure allows tides and currents to flow relatively unhindered, whereas the more solid foundations of a quay or the closely spaced piles of a wharf can act as a breakwater, and are consequently more liable to silting. Piers can range in size and complexity from a simple lightweight wooden structure to major structures extended over 1,600 m (5,200 ft). In American English, a pier may be synonymous with a dock."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Korfu_%28GR%29%2C_Korfu%2C_Altstadt_--_2018_--_1156.jpg/330px-Korfu_%28GR%29%2C_Korfu%2C_Altstadt_--_2018_--_1156.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pier","wikidata":"Q863454"},{"title":"Steel","blocks":[{"text":"Steel is an alloy of iron and carbon that demonstrates improved mechanical properties compared to the pure form of iron. Due to its high elastic modulus, yield strength, fracture strength and low raw material cost, steel is one of the most commonly manufactured materials in the world. Steel is used in structures, in bridges, infrastructure, tools, ships, trains, cars, bicycles, machines, electrical appliances, furniture, and weapons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%80%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%82.jpg/330px-%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%80%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steel","wikidata":"Q11427"},{"title":"Steel blue","blocks":[{"text":"Steel blue is a shade of blue color that resembles blue steel, i.e., steel which has been subjected to bluing for protection from rust. It is one of the less vibrant shades of blue, and is usually identified as a blue-gray color. An archaic term for it is chalybeous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Niter_and_Color_Case.jpg/330px-Niter_and_Color_Case.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steel_blue","wikidata":"Q1003676"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Fish_Market%2C_South_Street%2C_Manhattan_%28NYPL_b13668355-482702%29.jpg/500px-Fish_Market%2C_South_Street%2C_Manhattan_%28NYPL_b13668355-482702%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Fish_Market%2C_South_Street%2C_Manhattan_%28NYPL_b13668355-482702%29.jpg/1280px-Fish_Market%2C_South_Street%2C_Manhattan_%28NYPL_b13668355-482702%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Fish_Market%2C_South_Street%2C_Manhattan_%28NYPL_b13668355-482702%29.jpg/500px-Fish_Market%2C_South_Street%2C_Manhattan_%28NYPL_b13668355-482702%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Fish Market during the Great Depression","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFish_Market%2C_South_Street%2C_Manhattan_(NYPL_b13668355-482702).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/207-209-211_WATER_STREET_IN_CENTER_FOREGROUND_%28Note_identical_height_and_architectural_features%29_-_South_Street_Seaport_Museum%2C_207-211_Water_Street%2C_New_York%2C_New_York_County%2C_HABS_NY%2C31-NEYO%2C141-1.tif/lossy-page1-500px-thumbnail.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/207-209-211_WATER_STREET_IN_CENTER_FOREGROUND_%28Note_identical_height_and_architectural_features%29_-_South_Street_Seaport_Museum%2C_207-